<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="page1-utility-and-incomplete-info" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Utility, Lotteries, and Incomplete Information</title>

  <p>
    This chapter is where we let chance into our games. The first two
    sections set up the tools we'll need, so read them and keep moving. The
    real work starts in Section 4.3.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-4-1">
    <title>Section 4.1, Utility Functions and Lotteries</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 4.1.</alert> Two ideas:
    </p>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            A <term>utility function</term> <m>u(s)</m>, where <m>s</m> is an
            amount of money received. It is increasing
            (<m>u'(s) \gt 0</m>) and concave down (<m>u''(s) \lt 0</m>), so
            each additional dollar is worth a little less than the one before.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            A <term>lottery</term>: outcomes <m>x_1, \ldots, x_n</m> with
            probabilities <m>p_1, \ldots, p_n</m> summing to <m>1</m>. Its
            <term>expected value</term> is
            <me>E[x] = p_1 x_1 + \cdots + p_n x_n,</me>
            and its expected utility is
            <m>E[u(x)] = p_1 u(x_1) + \cdots + p_n u(x_n)</m>. The
            <term>expected utility principle</term> says you prefer the
            lottery with the higher expected utility.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-4-2">
    <title>Section 4.2, Buying Fire Insurance</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 4.2.</alert> A decision problem, not a game. To
      decide whether to insure, attach a probability and a payoff to each
      outcome<mdash/>fire or no fire, premium paid or not<mdash/>and compare
      the two options.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    We don't have anything to break down yet, because everything so far has
    been setting the scene for games in extensive form with <term>incomplete
    information</term>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Back in Chapter 1 we studied games in extensive form with <term>complete
    information</term>: whenever it was your turn, you knew every move made
    before yours, so you always knew where you were in the game tree. Now we
    allow chance into the tree. Once part of what has already happened was
    decided at random, or is hidden from you, you may not know which node
    you're standing at.
  </p>

  <p>
    Poker is the standard example. After the deal you know your own hand but
    not anyone else's. What you <em>can</em> do is assign probabilities to the
    hands your opponents might be holding. Section 4.3 makes that precise.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-4-3">
    <title>Section 4.3, Games in Extensive Form with Incomplete Information</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 4.3.</alert> Terminology to watch for:
    </p>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>Nature</term>: nodes assigned to chance rather than to a
            player. The probabilities on Nature's moves sum to <m>1</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>Information set</term>: a group of a player's nodes that the
            player cannot tell apart. The available moves are identical at
            every node of the set.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            A strategy must assign the same move to every node of an
            information set.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The <term>probability of a path</term>: the product of the
            probabilities of Nature's moves along it.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The <term>expected payoff</term> of a strategy profile: sum the
            payoff of each complete path times that path's probability.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
    <p>
      The last paragraph splits into two cases. If only Nature's nodes come
      before an information set, backward induction still works using expected
      value. If a player moved first, it doesn't, and we convert to normal
      form instead.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-4-1" iframe="question-form.html?section=4.1-4.3" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>
